      subCategories: ["Rangefinders", "GPS"],
      description: "Bushnell Golf didn't invent the laser rangefinder, but it made the category ubiquitous. Operating out of Overland Park, Kansas, as part of Vista Outdoor's portfolio, Bushnell has dominated golf distance measurement since introducing its first laser rangefinder in the early 2000s. The brand's devices—particularly the Pro X3 and Tour V5 series—are fixtures in tour player yardage books, favored for accuracy, durability, and consistent performance in variable weather. Bushnell's PinSeeker technology, which locks onto flagsticks and provides vibration feedback confirming measurement, became an industry standard that competitors rushed to replicate. The company expanded beyond lasers into GPS devices, speakers, and hybrid units combining both technologies, but rangefinders remain the core business. Bushnell's market position is built on reputation and reliability. Tour caddies trust Bushnell because the devices deliver accurate yardages round after round, year after year, surviving the physical abuse of constant bag transport and weather exposure. That professional credibility trickles down to amateurs willing to pay premium prices for equipment that won't fail mid-round. The brand's dominance has faced challenges from direct-to-consumer upstarts like Blue Tees and Precision Pro, which offer similar features at lower prices by cutting out retail markup. Bushnell has responded by expanding product tiers, introducing more affordable models while maintaining flagship devices for serious players. The company also invested in connected features like the Bushnell Golf app, which integrates GPS maps, round tracking, and device updates. Bushnell's ownership under Vista Outdoor—a sporting goods conglomerate that also owns ammunition and outdoor brands—provides resources for R&D and distribution but occasionally raises eyebrows among golfers uncomfortable with corporate parentage. Still, Bushnell remains the default choice for distance measurement, the industry standard against which all competitors are measured, and a rare example of a golf brand achieving true category dominance through product performance rather than marketing spend.",
