      allCategories: ["Bags & Accessories"],
      subCategories: ["Bags"],
      description: "Ghost Golf launched in 2020 as a direct-to-consumer brand focused on reimagining golf bags and accessories through the lens of minimalist design and premium materials. Founded in Irvine, California, the brand emerged during golf's pandemic boom, targeting a cohort of golfers frustrated by the aesthetic compromises in traditional golf equipment—bags that looked dated, overly branded, or visually cluttered. Ghost's flagship product, the Ghost Stand Bag, features clean lines, limited external branding, weatherproof materials, and a monochromatic color palette that feels more aligned with contemporary luggage or outdoor gear than country club traditionalism. The company emphasizes details: waterproof zippers, reinforced stitching, ergonomic strap systems, and thoughtful pocket organization designed for the modern golfer who carries rangefinders, portable speakers, and other tech alongside balls and tees. Pricing reflects the premium positioning, with stand bags around $350 and cart bags exceeding $400—significantly above mass-market options from brands like Titleist or Callaway, but positioned as investment pieces rather than disposable equipment. Ghost Golf's brand identity leans heavily on visual simplicity and lifestyle appeal, with marketing imagery that emphasizes architecture, design, and understated luxury rather than performance claims or tour validation. The brand expanded into accessories including headcovers, towels, and travel covers, all maintaining the same monochrome, logo-minimal aesthetic. Ghost Golf operates exclusively online, bypassing traditional retail distribution in favor of direct relationships with customers and controlling brand presentation. The company represents a broader trend in golf: challenger brands using design differentiation and community building to carve niches in categories long dominated by established equipment manufacturers. While Ghost lacks the scale or heritage of legacy bag brands, its growth reflects demand among younger, design-conscious golfers for equipment that looks like it belongs in their lives beyond the course—where form and function coexist without compromise.",
