      parentCompany: "Independent",
      category: "Bags & Accessories",
      allCategories: ["Bags & Accessories"],
      subCategories: ["Headcovers"],
      description: "Seamus Golf has carved a distinctive niche in golf accessories through handcrafted headcovers and goods that draw inspiration from Pacific Northwest heritage textiles and outdoor culture. Founded in Portland, Oregon in 2010, the brand built its reputation on wool headcovers featuring classic patterns like herringbone, tartan, and fair isle knits—designs that reference Scottish golf heritage while incorporating the craftsmanship aesthetic of Portland's maker community.\n\nThe company's signature products are driver and fairway wood headcovers constructed from durable wool blends, often in bold patterns with leather accents and details that reference vintage golf equipment. Each piece emphasizes visible craftsmanship—substantial materials, careful construction, and designs that improve with age and use. Beyond headcovers, Seamus produces golf bags, alignment stick covers, travel accessories, and apparel that maintain the brand's distinctive aesthetic: heritage patterns, quality materials, and design that bridges traditional golf and contemporary outdoor culture.\n\nSeamus Golf operates primarily direct-to-consumer through its website and Portland storefront, though the brand has expanded into select retail partnerships at premium golf shops and boutiques. The company has developed collaborations with courses, clubs, and other heritage brands, creating limited-edition headcovers that combine Seamus craftsmanship with partner-specific design elements. These collaborations tap into golf's growing appreciation for course-specific merchandise and locally-inspired goods.\n\nPricing reflects the premium materials and small-batch production, with headcovers typically ranging $60-100 and bags starting around $300. The brand appeals to golfers who view accessories as expressions of personal style and appreciate craftsmanship narratives—a growing segment as golf culture shifts toward appreciation for artisanal and heritage products. Seamus Golf represents the intersection of golf, outdoor culture, and maker aesthetics, offering products that reference golf's traditional roots while avoiding country club formality. The brand has become particularly popular among golfers seeking alternatives to logo-heavy tour brands or mass-produced accessories.",
